00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.130
So, Allan, if you'd be willing to jump in,

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.211
I know that that's not a light question

00:03:44.211 --> 00:03:46.580
by any means, but important.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.540
- No, but it's a real
question, it's where we are.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.813
As we kind of talked
before we hopped on live.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.280
I think everybody is understanding

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.793
we're in a world that
we hadn't seen before.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.920
So we're all trying to navigate it.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:08.890
For me, we are approaching a
year since kind of locked down.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.370
And I remember literally
March 10th last year

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.787
was the day it really kind of hit me

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:18.221
because I was with our
team with the Knicks.

00:04:18.221 --> 00:04:23.221
They literally stopped games
and this on March 10th.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.290
And so, I'm just kinda
going back and first of all,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.090
I can't believe it's been a year for one,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.373
and then it's like what was
I doing at the beginning?

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.300
I think for me, I have been
to such a busy lifestyle.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.313
Again, it gave me a chance to
spend time at home reflect.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.540
So I kind of had this dual perspective,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.830
one was like, I was
really kind of relieved

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.448
that I could just have
a real reason to be home

00:04:53.448 --> 00:04:58.448
and not be anywhere else
and engage with my family.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.610
But on the other side, you see the pain

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.150
that so many are going through

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.872
and the pain that you're kind of sensing

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:09.772
that you don't know how to
kind of deal with and navigate,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.060
because I feel like as a nation

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.650
some layers finally
got peeled back, right.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.180
It's almost like we're
kind of getting to the root

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.957
and discovery of who we really are

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:28.870
and not who we're portraying
to be as a nation.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.363
And didn't look pretty,

00:05:31.406 --> 00:05:34.240
and so we're trying to figure out,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.858
it's almost like living
in a house with a brother

00:05:36.858 --> 00:05:41.520
or sister that you still
trying to learn and know

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.320
and you find out some ugliness,
but you're still family.

00:05:46.504 --> 00:05:49.780
How do we navigate that
and push through it

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.160
deal with the ups and downs

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.860
which I love this conversation
of stress and resilience

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.900
because it's not just
the physical tiredness,

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.612
it's the emotional, it's knowing
that I have to endure this,

00:06:01.612 --> 00:06:03.153
and I just have to.

00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:07.343
So one side it's like, I
know I have to keep pushing.

00:06:07.343 --> 00:06:09.860
I have no choice, but on
the other side it's like,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.233
why do we have to keep
going over this again?

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:19.383
And I call myself an eternal optimist.

00:06:21.134 --> 00:06:22.334
I have always felt like,

00:06:23.982 --> 00:06:27.611
at my perspective is
eternity, or my no God

00:06:27.611 --> 00:06:30.197
that has an ultimate
plan and a destination.

00:06:30.197 --> 00:06:32.070
So I try to keep that in mind

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.270
and kinda work myself back.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:37.193
But when you get to where you are,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.020
it's a struggle, it's a fight

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.370
because not everybody
has the best intentions

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.603
and in different experiences.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.500
So it's always a challenge
but at the end of the day,

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.523
I just try to remind
myself of why I'm here.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.614
And that's not always to be comfortable

00:06:58.614 --> 00:07:00.011
whereas to make a difference.

00:07:00.011 --> 00:07:04.260
- Yeah, now that, that higher
purpose in a lot of ways.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.333
And so meeting at the party,

00:07:05.333 --> 00:07:07.120
one of the things that really resonated

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.230
with me was just the idea of in a sense

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:11.600
like it was a blessing in disguise

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.420
to be able to have to
deal with these things

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.620
but that doesn't make
it easy by any means.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.046
Like you said, it's things
that were already there

00:07:20.046 --> 00:07:21.960
that we have to address head on

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.960
but there's almost no escape.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.912
Because now, I mean in this,
there is always a way to escape

00:07:26.912 --> 00:07:29.870
but when it's right there
in front of your face,

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.263
these are the realities,
then we might have to face.

00:07:32.263 --> 00:07:34.550
- I think the thing about escaping

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.960
like it seems like this
conversation brought escaping

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.313
is not really escaping.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.590
But escaping is not
really dealing with it.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.129
So you're not really escaping.

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:46.087
- (laughs) Very true, yeah.

00:07:46.087 --> 00:07:49.210
Yeah, and I think sometimes people think

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.010
it's still comes back like you said.

00:07:52.146 --> 00:07:54.833
Rajita, what about you in this moment?

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:00.090
- A lot of what Allan was
saying I was resonating with it

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.420
I can't believe it's been a year,

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:06.050
it feels like it's gone
by and it's both fast

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:09.160
and slow at the same
time, there were aspects

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:12.220
that I appreciated having moments to think

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.160
about things without feeling rushed

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.293
cutting out all travel,
which was a blessing.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:24.160
But, it also really uncovered
a lot of who we are,

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:29.050
challenged us in ways we didn't know

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:33.690
how we would react as both
as a community, as a nation

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:37.960
but just in myself too,

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.370
we were challenged in different ways.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.403
And so I think it's been
hard from that perspective.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.213
You have to relearn some
things of how to function

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:54.811
in a very restrictive environment.

00:08:54.811 --> 00:08:59.540
Your social network is not
there for you in the same way,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.660
so you sort of have to find a new way

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.670
of sort of connecting with people.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.730
And that's a more conscientious way

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:08.450
of putting oneself out

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.683
which you can't take things for granted.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.340
And then from a work
perspective, it got very stressed

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.515
because there was lots
of sort of restrictions

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:21.910
and there were worries about
how would we get things done

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.690
that we have to get things get done.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:29.690
And then taking care of the
people around me, my family,

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.930
personally, it was hard for family members

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:36.490
who one couldn't see,
who were ill and sick.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:39.763
So there there's that whole
dimension of it as well.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:46.010
But it's been tough so much more tough

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.610
for other people who have lost loved ones.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:53.010
I had illness in the family

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.810
but not not actually lost somebody.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:59.900
And so I feel a level of gratitude

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.470
for what I could sort of manage

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:09.410
and even one was challenged
in different ways.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.830
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.180
In several ways, it
sounds like you're talking

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.860
about just the mixture of
emotions that come to the loss

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.630
and then some of the resilience
in the midst of the stress.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:19.997
And I really like how you brought up

00:10:19.997 --> 00:10:20.843
the learning piece as well

00:10:20.843 --> 00:10:23.480
because I think that's
been key in a lot of ways.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:27.080
And some of that has just been
having a willingness to learn

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.600
I would say too.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.644
And with learning, always
there are always mistakes.

00:10:30.644 --> 00:10:32.640
I mean, I'm sure Allan
you could talk about that

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.650
from a sports standpoint,
Rajita from a life standpoint

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.660
but really being willing to learn

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:38.704
and have those mistakes happen,

00:10:38.704 --> 00:10:40.160
I know for me with my students,

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.150
a lot of it has been talking

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:44.260
about basically resetting goals, say okay,

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:45.780
these are the goals I have in mind.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.500
This is a pandemic, I can't
actually meet those goals,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.790
I need to kind of reset and set new goals.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.130
And then I still can't meet
those goals so I reset.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.240
So I mean, it's really a
cyclical iterative process

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.183
and not being so hard on ourselves

00:10:58.183 --> 00:10:59.855
that we don't give ourselves
that room to learn.

00:10:59.855 --> 00:11:01.160
Because as you both mentioned

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.980
this is completely new for all of us.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:05.670
I mean, unless any of us
were alive 100 years ago

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.750
we haven't experienced this before.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.460
So there was no template
for how to go through it

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:13.070
but we do have principles
which we can apply as well.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.610
And so I think that's
gonna be a really rich part

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.910
of this conversation as well.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:19.770
And so, I mean, I know you
both already have had passion

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.877
about these aspects of
stress and resilience.

00:11:22.877 --> 00:11:25.040
And so anything you'd like
to share just in terms

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.390
of how you've applied that passion

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.098
over the last year in this pandemic

00:11:29.098 --> 00:11:31.860
and how other things
that you've had to shift

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:32.840
even in the framework

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.520
for how you've approached
some of these things.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.800
- Yeah, no I think the challenge

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.923
really got you to think about

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.960
one's own sort of sanity first.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:50.480
Obviously, at your own personal level

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.100
and acceptance of what we don't know,

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:57.240
because I remember having this
conversation with colleagues

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.990
about whether this is an acute
trauma that we're facing.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:04.900
And I just said I was on the
other side of it and felt,

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:07.980
and continued to feel that
this wasn't an acute trauma,

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:10.930
like you have a disaster, a hurricane

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.440
and of course you deal with

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.010
the consequences of the hurricane

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:19.010
but the hurricane leaves,
this is chronic, relentless,

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:23.030
and not knowing at least back
in March and April and May

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:25.330
and through the fall not
knowing when it would end

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:29.960
if it would end in a
way that would be even

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.450
at all salvageable as we come out of it.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:35.100
And then all other
layers, which I know Nii

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.299
you referred to in your first episode,

00:12:37.299 --> 00:12:38.617
and we may talk about it

00:12:38.617 --> 00:12:41.980
but there were other things that got

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:43.790
the onion sort of pulled back

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.050
as Allan you were referring to who we are

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:49.750
and different ways and
challenging that also came about.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.000
So I think just for me personally,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.590
there was a lot of acceptance and focusing

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.050
and staying with the acceptance

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.993
and staying with the multiple
emotions that come up.

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:04.680
I appreciated having time to
exercise more for example,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.550
to have control over your schedule

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.940
in a way that you could do those.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:14.330
So there were some
nuggets of good I suppose

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.870
in one's own sort of existence

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:20.963
but it came with a lot of
these other mixtures, so yeah.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.570
- Yeah, I'm curious about
one piece you brought up too

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:26.980
before you pivot back to Allan

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:30.260
but just this idea of it
not being an acute stressor

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:32.453
but something that was much more chronic,

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:35.803
how do our brains respond to that

00:13:35.803 --> 00:13:38.875
and what's the impact on
that on us as individuals?

00:13:38.875 --> 00:13:41.531
Like how should we be
thinking about the nature

00:13:41.531 --> 00:13:43.670
of the stressor itself and
how we approach things?

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.124
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:13:45.124 --> 00:13:48.620
And it really gets to the
heart of sort of what is stress

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:52.188
and how do we conceptualize it

00:13:52.188 --> 00:13:54.653
and then study it and then deal with it.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.520
Acute stressors are things
that have a beginning

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.773
and an end, very discreet.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.630
And they challenge us and
it can be controllable

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.380
or uncontrollable, of course,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.540
and uncontrollable really challenge us

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:13.540
in ways that that can probe in a risk

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:17.890
or very quick action to save
us and that kind of thing

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.940
but then there's a recovery from it.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:25.180
And there is a coming
down of our physiologic

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:27.070
as well as our mental processes,

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:29.225
sort of whether it's relief,

00:14:29.225 --> 00:14:31.510
whether it's sort of a
sense of accomplishment,

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:32.400
whether it's learning

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:34.742
as you pointed to learning
is a big part of it

00:14:34.742 --> 00:14:37.040
adapting to the situation

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.267
sort of putting it together to know it

00:14:39.267 --> 00:14:41.080
but I'm not gonna do that again,

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.950
or I'm not gonna feel that,

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:45.810
and sometimes making
you not growing from it.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:46.860
Sort of making decisions,

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:50.690
like, I think we're gonna do
this differently the next time.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.380
So you have room to manage that sometimes,

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:56.030
sometimes uncontrollable
stress can be ones

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:59.210
that really challenge you in ways that

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:04.650
put you on the spot to learn in major ways

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.300
and you have to be open for that.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:12.230
The chronic, relentless
is one that unfortunately,

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:14.220
a number of people in our community

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.580
already know what that is,
not in the way of the pandemic

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:21.523
but there is a lot of
chronic, relentless stress.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.140
If you were about to
get evicted, for example

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:29.040
or you don't know where the
next paycheck is gonna come

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.990
or whether you will have the rent money

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:35.950
and that just like one example of it

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.220
but this had those qualities

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.910
your own person may not be okay.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.440
You might get affected by this illness

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:44.930
or somebody you love might be,

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.800
you might lose someone who's already sick.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:51.303
There's just a lot of
uncertainty in there.

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:55.270
And like I said, there
wasn't a clear message

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:59.410
about how we were gonna come out of it.

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:03.900
So any ways in which
socially we could get to

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.640
sort of gaining control those things

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:08.000
weren't happening in a very concise way.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.610
Some States were better than others,

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.890
and we don't have to get
into the social side of it

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:13.520
but just generally thinking

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:17.110
about a stressor is it
was a chronic, relentless,

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.280
uncontrollable stress for a long time.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.300
- Yeah that's very, very true.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.130
And Allan you hint into this as well

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:27.300
but how have you been
trying to address that

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.303
both in your own life
and in the communities

00:16:29.303 --> 00:16:31.010
that you're involved in?

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:34.370
- Mostly just doing this, just talking,

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:35.840
just listening to people,

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:40.180
I think anytime you go through something

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:46.280
I think traumatic, I think
being in a support group

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:50.160
being able to express
and listen to connect

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.710
with people who are going
through something similar

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:55.920
that's what I found a lot of strength

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:00.120
and recovery and just connection with.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:04.160
Obviously, technology
allow that to happen.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:09.133
So, you kind of get Zoom
fatigue but for the most part,

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:15.730
I felt like as much energy
that got drained from us

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:22.000
I felt like I got energy
from certain conversations.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.750
Sometimes you get on prayer calls

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:28.440
with people and just talk.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.116
And even though like
people are immobilized

00:17:31.116 --> 00:17:34.080
and they're sitting in the
same physical situation

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:39.080
or same physical place
you can kind of be moved

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:42.310
in a different way by having
a conversation with someone,

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.270
connecting with someone,

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:48.770
just being there to listen to people.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.450
So I think that's one of
the things we've been doing

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:55.650
with kind of our support
groups, mentoring,

00:17:55.650 --> 00:18:00.330
leadership development,
just really listening

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:05.330
to a lot of young people,
letting them voice their concerns

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:09.483
voice their aspirations,

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.230
just to getting their feedback

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:15.540
on what's going on in their
world and how they interpret

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.890
and perceive things that
are going on socially.

00:18:18.758 --> 00:18:21.193
I think a lot of people haven't felt

00:18:21.193 --> 00:18:24.860
like they had a voice and
they hadn't been validated.

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:28.723
So I think that was
the primary way for me,

00:18:30.197 --> 00:18:33.830
kind of in a in a macro
sense to just deal with,

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:38.250
the stress of everything,
connecting with people.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:43.250
and within myself doing the
same thing, reflecting a lot

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:50.580
just thinking about what's
most important in life

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.313
most important relationships.

00:18:53.437 --> 00:18:56.670
And just trying to keep it on that plane.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:57.503
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:18:57.503 --> 00:19:00.050
I think there's the element of
community that you brought up

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:02.430
just really giving people the space

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:03.740
to stay engaged in community.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.780
And like you said, re-centering too,

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:07.930
just re-centering in terms of
the strength that we can build

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:10.220
from community and
re-centering spiritually,

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.030
I think as well as
strength that we can have

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:13.730
from God in those moments as well.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:16.248
One piece that I was curious
about from both of you

00:19:16.248 --> 00:19:17.710
based on what you've just said

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:21.285
is just if you've seen
specific strengths emerge

00:19:21.285 --> 00:19:25.050
that weren't necessarily as evident before

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:29.700
as people have gone through
this chronic stress.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:34.700
- I think the positive
on the strength of this,

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:38.000
what happened was is related
to what Allan is saying,

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.060
which is that it forced us to think

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:43.070
about community in many ways

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:45.610
when people have been busy in their life

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:48.120
or doing the things that they need to do

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:51.050
or struggling and managing things.

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:53.930
And haven't had a chance to
take a step back or reflect.

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:57.290
And this forced us to do
that in top situations

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:00.843
as well as in situations where
you didn't know what to do.

00:20:02.481 --> 00:20:04.880
And it allowed us to dig into that sense

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:09.770
of sort of reaching out,
supporting one another using Zoom.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:11.300
You heard a lot of that using Zoom

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.550
or phones or whatever other technology

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:19.320
or just even connecting with
your church groups or whoever,

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.990
even you just the neighbors
not that you were seeing them,

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:25.960
but watching them and then connecting

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:30.340
through some media modality

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.400
it really allowed you to sort of

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:36.260
get into that connectivity issue

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.283
that is so important for humans.

00:20:40.198 --> 00:20:41.630
We are social animals

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.600
and it was really a good
way to hone into that.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:45.767
But for a lot of people

00:20:45.767 --> 00:20:50.180
and a lot of young people
they really connect

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.490
but they miss the other piece, the contact

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.063
and you hear that from them.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.320
And so actually a lot of reassurance

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.993
and sort of teaching of
patience which is harder,

00:21:04.372 --> 00:21:06.370
so I think even that got challenged

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.570
while that is a strength

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:11.010
that's perhaps something
that we weren't aware

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.870
of how much investment
we need to make in that.

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:18.820
I think we got a chance to
see this, the community not...

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:22.210
you need to fabric not being as strong

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:23.790
as it could have been.

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:24.890
- Yeah, that's really true.

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:28.220
I've had lots of yelling
conversations with neighbors

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:31.260
across the street that
just did in a different way

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:32.940
but it's funny cause you can see

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:36.534
that tension and that desire to,

00:21:36.534 --> 00:21:37.869
as people are out doing yard work

00:21:37.869 --> 00:21:38.959
and they're getting closer together

00:21:38.959 --> 00:21:40.500
then all of a sudden it's like, oh,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:41.960
I forgot we have to social distance.

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:42.793
It's like yearning to urinate

00:21:42.793 --> 00:21:45.290
and it was just starting to bubble up.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.163
So I think you're exactly
right, it's teaching us a lot.

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:51.773
- Yeah, I mean I think
it definitely gave us

00:21:52.736 --> 00:21:56.820
a better appreciation for just nature,

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:01.700
just doing the things that
are just natural to us

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:04.823
just walking, talking, exercising,

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:08.593
those things that just help us be whole.

00:22:09.530 --> 00:22:14.530
I think that what got
strengthened is as a thing

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:18.260
which you kind of say it's our
ability to think and reflect

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:21.010
it's our ability to think about things.

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.820
So to really think about
it a more deeply, right.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:28.030
And it gave us a little bit
more of a self-awareness

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:29.860
like again, that we didn't like

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:34.720
but we needed to know within
ourselves or just other people.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.013
I know, that's what
they're really thinking.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:41.190
Now I see what they're really
thinking, I thought it,

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:43.277
but now I'm seeing it, they said it

00:22:43.277 --> 00:22:44.921
and we were talking about it

00:22:44.921 --> 00:22:48.990
and we're being forced to talk
about uncomfortable things

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.870
and things that I didn't
know were not okay to say.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:57.870
And so those things I think
helped us in a lot of ways.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.900
Now we gotta apply it

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:05.690
and just continue to be
mindful of those things.

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:10.170
But yeah, I agree, I think
those things did help.

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:12.420
Just the idea of thinking,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.600
and I think you made a
great point too as well

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:16.120
'cause I have seven children.

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:18.210
So I see the progression
of an eight year old

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:19.800
all the way up to a 21 year old.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:24.590
And you realize that the older you are,

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:28.730
the more you're okay with
kind of dialogue and thinking

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:31.700
and that type of engagement
with the younger you are.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:34.960
I just want some physical
engagement, you know what I mean?

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:39.960
So you really see this natural progression

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:43.430
of how we live and how we develop

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.110
and how we really do
need the young people,

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:50.800
the older we are to have
some social engagement

00:23:52.101 --> 00:23:53.653
it's extremely important.

00:23:54.848 --> 00:23:59.650
And that's why I'm praying
that everything can quicken

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.010
so that people can kind
of just get out again

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:03.563
and feel that sense.

00:24:04.464 --> 00:24:06.584
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:24:06.584 --> 00:24:08.980
Rajita, do you have suggestions for people

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:11.600
in terms of that piece
that Allan just brought out

00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:13.590
especially for him, you
mentioned the young people

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:16.050
like still trying to have those spaces

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.303
of physical connection,
that's so important.

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:22.850
- Well, the physical connection is tough

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:25.400
because you have to at some
point make the contact,

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:29.830
but I think Zoom goes a long way.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:33.910
I think people saying using the
different sensory modalities

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:36.380
to connect with other people

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.780
I think that's creative
ways in which teachers

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:41.913
and others have reached out to do that.

00:24:43.670 --> 00:24:46.900
I've heard for example,
getting kids together

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:49.579
for a joint movement things

00:24:49.579 --> 00:24:53.130
whether it's joint dance
workshops or singing together

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.160
and yoga together and
some physical activity

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:59.010
in that way has gone on

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:01.280
which has been important and
the kids have enjoyed it.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:06.280
And again, depending on the age
group, I think that matters.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:10.086
I do think that the other piece of this

00:25:10.086 --> 00:25:12.160
and maybe you were gonna get to it,

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:16.640
is that the need for connection
around the difficulties

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:19.280
that have gone on in
this past year as well,

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.160
because I think the children particularly

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:23.420
how you connect on that
is really important

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.480
but there has been such a
tremendous amount of loss

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:30.810
in many ways even though we are talking

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.520
about we got challenged
and we tried to figure out

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:38.385
how to cope with it and managed
to strengthen those aspects.

00:25:38.385 --> 00:25:40.000
There was a sense of loss.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:43.410
You lost your way of living
and your way of being

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:47.050
and at the most, just at the top level

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:50.180
and then there was lost
in very physical ways

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:53.770
family members losing, I
mean loss had just multiple

00:25:53.770 --> 00:25:55.640
levels in the pain related to that.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:58.470
And I felt like we don't
talk enough about that

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.679
because we'd spent so much
time wanting to be as Americans

00:26:01.679 --> 00:26:05.450
we like to be upbeat and happy

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:07.970
and oh we're all great and resilient

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:13.480
and not perhaps paying as much attention

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:14.577
to what's coming apart.

00:26:14.577 --> 00:26:17.180
- Yeah, that's really important.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:19.365
So I'm curious, how do you
suggest for both of you

00:26:19.365 --> 00:26:21.410
that we deal with that loss

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:24.480
in terms of even just
acknowledging that it's there

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.760
but then having the
tools to move through it

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:27.838
and to build resilience?

00:26:27.838 --> 00:26:29.160
Because I think in some ways,

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:30.860
sometimes there are misconceptions

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:33.463
about what resilience
really is and what it isn't.

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.190
So what would you say to people in terms

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:39.627
of what are appropriate
ways to address those things

00:26:39.627 --> 00:26:42.010
and what things should
we be trying to avoid

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:44.320
in terms of how we approach it?

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:46.560
- Yeah, I mean I'll just start

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.790
I think what Allan said
is really important.

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:50.660
I think the support and listening

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:54.010
and talking is one of
our very natural tools

00:26:55.041 --> 00:26:59.040
but really going to
where the dark side is,

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:02.690
where the darker aspects of
it to not be afraid of that

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:06.890
to reach out about pain and
sadness and about distress

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:12.360
and to stay with it because
we wanna go very quickly

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:13.630
to how do you cope with that?

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:15.270
And what do you do with it?

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.590
And there's a natural
way in which that can

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:21.770
as the risk to promote
avoidance in that way.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:26.620
And so I think the knowing pain

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:31.170
there's a lot of beautiful
historical context of you.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.920
If you know pain, it
gives you the answers,

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:37.640
it shows you what path you need to take.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:40.960
And so pain and distress
teaches us as well.

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:45.080
And my own personal belief
is, and what I see around

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:48.400
is that we don't pay as
much attention to it.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:51.800
So there's a risk that people
who are facing the loss

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:56.010
in that very deep way,
tend to put it aside,

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:58.450
not talk about it because
others are not paying

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:01.710
as much attention to it and
have moved on from that.

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:07.520
And so we do a lot of work
around on the trauma of things

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.460
and how to accentuate it so that in fact,

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:17.030
not to have people suffer
but to know their pain.

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:19.663
And there's a difference in those aspects.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:21.687
- That's really important.

00:28:21.687 --> 00:28:23.320
- Yeah, that's really good.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.900
I mean, that blessed me, right there Nii,

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:27.370
because I'll be honest with you

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:29.290
I didn't really have a good answer

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:33.837
but when Dr. Rajita
